From 506311aff1ecf2e79b781b7693145d346f2ec9cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 07:25:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] renderengine: Share the KawaseDarkmoon downsample chain
 between a layer's blurs

A layer can need several blurs of the same input: its background at one
radius, and blur regions at other radii. Each one previously ran the whole
Darkmoon pipeline, including the downsample passes.

The downsample passes do not depend on the blur radius. Only the upsample
passes and their step do. The levels for one input and rectangle are now
computed once and reused by every blur of the layer. Each radius runs only its
own upsample passes, which removes about half the passes for every blur after
the first.

The shared levels live in their own cache, and the upsample targets in a
second one, so an upsample pass never overwrites a level another radius still
needs. Only one prepared input is in use at a time. The layer blur block
prepares one, uses it for all of its blurs, and drops it before the next layer.
A generation check makes an input that has been overtaken fail safely instead
of reading the wrong levels.

The arithmetic is the same as in generateTemporaryImage, so the output is
unchanged. Protected content keeps the existing path. Filters that do not
implement the new interface keep their behaviour.

Signed-off-by: Omkar Parte <88646966+RAM-UNLOK@users.noreply.github.com>
